   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\">\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><a name=\"m_5063983002461043620__MailEndCompose\" rel=\"nofollow\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Hi Vincent,<u></u><u></u></span></a></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">The short answer is yes.  The function compare.multi.evol.rates will accept either shape data or a matrix of other\r\n continuous variables. If you use the latter, one may then compare rates for uni- or multivariate data. The help file for the function describes the input format.\r\n<u></u><u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">All the best,<u></u><u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Dean<u></u><u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">Dr. Dean C. Adams<u></u><u></u></span></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">D